# Local dev config
# settings.local.json is per-developer by convention — .claude/settings.json is the shared
# file and is committed. Keeping the local one out avoids a permission grant made on one
# machine turning into a permission grant for the whole team.
.env
.env.local
.claude/settings.local.json

# Build artifacts
view/out/
view/dist/
*.vsix
*.tsbuildinfo

# Derived memory index — rebuildable, so restore it by rebuilding, never from a commit.
memory/index/*
!memory/index/.gitkeep

# Session memory — one pipeline run's checkpoints, gate artifacts and band state.
# Ephemeral by design; committing it would replay one developer's half-finished run in
# somebody else's checkout.
memory/sessions/*
!memory/sessions/.gitkeep

# Decision logs ARE committed. They record what was decided and why, including entries
# that were later superseded, and that history is only useful to the team if the team
# can read it. An agent that cannot see a rejected decision proposes it again.


# Eval run output — timestamped result files, regenerated on every run.
evals/runs/

# Knowledge pipeline: the whole corpus ships, originals included.
#
# inbox/, extracted/, distilled/ and index/ are all committed, so a clone of this
# repository is a complete knowledge base and an install from GitHub behaves exactly
# like an install from a local directory. That equivalence is the point: a plugin that
# works on the machine it was built on and silently lacks half its corpus everywhere
# else is the harder failure to notice, because nothing reports it.
#
# Two consequences, both deliberate and both worth knowing before adding a second
# client's material:
#
#   Size. inbox/ is roughly 57 MB of PDFs, decks and images. Git history is permanent,
#   so every clone from here on carries that weight whether or not it needs it.
#
#   Scope. The corpus is no longer scoped to one project, and the confidentiality
#   boundary is now whoever can read this repository. That is acceptable while the
#   repository is private and holds one client's material. It stops being acceptable
#   the moment a second client's documents land here, because a query would match
#   across both and no mechanism separates them.

